  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "Find where the tangent to the curve $y=2{x}^{5}-8x$ at the point $(1,-6)$ intersects the $y$-axis.",
  "worked_solution": "Let $f(x)=2{x}^{5}-8x.$ Let's first calculate the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{5}-8x)=10{x}^{4}-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the slope of the tangent at our point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={f}^{\u2032}(1)=10(1)^{4}-8=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the tangent is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y+6 & =2(x-1) \\\\ y & =2x-2-6 \\\\ y & =2x-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the tangent line intercepts the $y$-axis, we set $x=0$ and solve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2(0)-8 \\\\ y & =-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point of intersection is $(0,-8).$",
